BEGIN_PGML Suppose we color each vertex of a graph so that adjacent vertices always have different colors. The smallest number of colors needed to do this is called the chromatic number of the graph. Find the chromatic number for each graph.
[`G_1\text{:}`] [_]{3}{5}; [`G_2\text{:}`] [_]{2}{5}; [`G_3\text{:}`] [_]{3}{5}; [`G_4\text{:}`] [_]{2}{5}.
Note: If the graphs represented friendships between people, then the chromatic number would tell us the minimum number of groups we would need if we wanted to divide up everyone into groups of people who were not yet friends.
END_PGML
